The loop every applied ML engineer actually runs. Theory paths teach algorithms; this module teaches the sequence — and the discipline that keeps results honest.

Work the procedure below on a real dataset, in order — the sequence is the lesson.
1. **The applied loop: frame, data, baseline, evaluate, iterate, ship** — Frame the problem, gather data, build a baseline, evaluate, iterate, and ship — with experiment discipline at every step.
2. **Baselines first: why simple models set the bar** — Baselines turn ‘is this good?’ into a measurable comparison.
3. **Train/validation/test splits that survive contact with reality** — Tuning against the test set turns it into a second validation set; touching it once gives the only honest number.
4. **Experiment discipline: seeds, versions, run logs** — Reproducibility and fair comparison need seeds, versions, and logs.
5. **Knowing when to stop tuning** — When further iterations stop moving the validation metric meaningfully, or the gains cost more than they are worth — then ship or reframe.
6. **Close the loop** — apply every step above to one real dataset, and write down what you tried, what moved the result, and what you would do next.

Module sources:
- [scikit-learn Documentation](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/) — User guide + API reference; the canonical classical-ML library.
- [Google Machine Learning Crash Course](https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course) — Google's free applied-ML course: framing, features, and evaluation.

