Evaluation as engineering, not ceremony: which metric, which slice, which mistakes cost money — and the discipline of reporting on a held-out set exactly once.

Work the procedure below on a real dataset, in order — the sequence is the lesson.
1. **Choosing the metric that matches the business cost** — Match it to the business cost of each mistake type — the metric is a contract with the stakeholder, not a default.
2. **Confusion matrices read properly** — The off-diagonal cells are the story — they show exactly how the model fails.
3. **Precision/recall tradeoffs on imbalanced data** — A majority-class predictor scores 99% on 1% positives while learning nothing — precision/recall expose the emptiness.
4. **Calibration: when probabilities must mean something** — Whenever the numbers drive money or thresholds — a predicted 0.7 must happen about 70% of the time, or expected-value math breaks.
5. **Error analysis by slice, not by average** — Breaking errors down by segment (cohort, region, input type) instead of one average — averages hide the slice where the model fails users.
6. **Close the loop** — apply every step above to one real dataset, and write down what you tried, what moved the result, and what you would do next.

