The adaptation workflow: curate instruction data, adapt efficiently with LoRA, and measure honestly — because fine-tuning can also make models worse.

Work the procedure below on a real dataset, in order — the sequence is the lesson.
1. **What pretraining actually teaches a model** — Broad statistical patterns of language and the world from massive text — general representations, not task skills.
2. **Instruction tuning: data curation matters most** — The model learns the behavior distribution you show it — quality and diversity of examples dominate quantity.
3. **LoRA and PEFT: adapting without full retraining** — LoRA: train low-rank update matrices with base weights frozen.
4. **Training runs: schedules, checkpoints, resuming** — Schedules, checkpoints, resumability, and logged metrics — runs fail; discipline makes them survivable.
5. **Evaluating before and after: did tuning help?** — Evaluate before and after on your task set — tuning can make models worse, and only measurement tells.
6. **Close the loop** — apply every step above to one real dataset, and write down what you tried, what moved the result, and what you would do next.

