Serving what you tuned: batching, quantized deployment, and the per-request cost math that decides whether the system is viable.

Work the procedure below on a real dataset, in order — the sequence is the lesson.
1. **Batching and throughput vs latency** — Bigger batches raise throughput but add latency per request — tune to your SLA, not to max throughput.
2. **VLLM-class serving at a glance** — Paged attention and continuous batching — far higher serving efficiency than naive implementations.
3. **Quantized deployment: quality checks after compression** — Quality on your eval set — compression is only free if the metrics say so.
4. **Caching and request routing** — Caching kills repeat cost; routing sends easy queries to cheap models — both directly cut the per-request bill.
5. **Cost modeling per thousand requests** — Tokens in/out × price plus infra overhead, at your real traffic mix — the math that decides viability.
6. **Close the loop** — apply every step above to one real dataset, and write down what you tried, what moved the result, and what you would do next.

